WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
So the title of this video is kind of a joke on&nbsp;
my part. Because before the 11th and 12th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.560
no one had ever thought of the slavs as being&nbsp;
very maritime oriented. This was due to the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.680
that for most of their early history they didn’t&nbsp;
live near large bodies of water. In the North the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.560
Baltic, Finno Ugric and Norse tribes were near&nbsp;
the seas, in the south you had varying nomadic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.680
and turkic groups like the Pechenegs or Khazars&nbsp;
and in the balkans along the adriatic coast there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.560
were still the remnantses of Rome in the form of&nbsp;
Latin cities that controlled the seafaring lanes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.080
in that area. And it was in between all of that,&nbsp;
that the early slavs lived mostly landlocked.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.920
However, this is not to say that the early&nbsp;
Slavs were completely incopetant when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.080
came to sailing, after all they had a&nbsp;
lot of experience navigating the large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.240
waterways of Eastern Europe from the&nbsp;
Polesian marshes to the large Dnieper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.800
Dniester, Bug, Vistula, Danube rivers&nbsp;
and all the tributaries around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.600
it is safe to say that the slavs where well&nbsp;
versed when it came to freshwater navigation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.560
plus they most certainly picked up new sailing&nbsp;
skills from their neighboring tribes as attested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.120
to by the many forieng borrowed words in the&nbsp;
slavic language when it comes to sailing. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.480
were even accounts of Slavs raiding Greek islands&nbsp;
during the migration period so they most certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.520
had the means for at least some sort of seafaring.
But with all that said it would be hard not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.560
mention the fact that early Slavs in general have&nbsp;
always been a ground fighting force with whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.080
sailing experience they had being used more for&nbsp;
transport or trading than for military purposes.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.000
Therefore the Avar siege of Constantinople&nbsp;
in 626 is so interesting. You’re probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.320
asking right now what do the Avars&nbsp;
and their siege of a city have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.960
anything with a quote unquote “Slavic&nbsp;
Navy” but before we get to that lets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.760
first introduce the background to this quite&nbsp;
historic and kind of anti climactic siege.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.280
The Avars have been in control of Pannonia&nbsp;
since their conquest of the Gepids in 566/7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.560
and the Lombard migration into Italy in&nbsp;
568. Ever since then the Avar Khaganate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.640
was a constant thorn in the Byzantine&nbsp;
back, raiding into the Balkan Byzantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.960
territory while also displacing a lot of&nbsp;
people groups, mostly the Slavs, who moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.000
towards the Balkans causing more problems for&nbsp;
the Byzantines. Not to mention the Avar exacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.720
of payments from the Byzantines to stop many of&nbsp;
these raids. The last of these payments before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:25.760
the siege of Constantinople was negotiated in&nbsp;
623 and consisted of 200k solidi, which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.480
around 50k more solidi than what Attila the Hun&nbsp;
negotiated with the Romans in 447. In fact it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.240
was stated by Nicephorus that the payment was “an&nbsp;
amount never previously paid to the barbarians”.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.080
So, considering that the Avars&nbsp;
had this very lucrative 200k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.227 --> 00:02:44.560
solidi peace deal with the Byzantines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.080
it begs the question of why did they decide&nbsp;
to attack Constantinople just 3 years later?

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.600
Well for that we have to turn to&nbsp;
something that has been sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.200
named the last war of Antiquity, that is&nbsp;
the Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.000
The Persians had seen much success&nbsp;
during the first half of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.760
mainly due to a power struggle within Byzantium.&nbsp;
The Sasanians took over Syria, Egypt, Armenia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.360
and parts of Anatolia. However, when the new&nbsp;
and much famed Emperor Heraclius managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.360
finally consolidate power within Byzantium and&nbsp;
instal the Heraclian reforms ending in 622,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:27.120
the tide of the war started to change. Heraclius&nbsp;
negotiated the aforementioned 200k solidi peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.440
deal with the Avars in 623 and turned&nbsp;
all of his attention on the Persians.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.240
Now against the entire might of the newly&nbsp;
consolidated Byznatine Empire the Persians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.040
were on the back foot, losing a couple of&nbsp;
battles and parts of Anatolia and Armenia.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.320
Seeing the tide of war turn in the Byznatine&nbsp;
favor, the last ditch actions of the Persians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.880
seem to have been to ally themselves with the&nbsp;
Avars and persuade them to break their peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.760
treaty with the Byzantines. The Persians most&nbsp;
likely promised the Avars all of the Byzantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.400
Balkan provinces as they themselves didn’t&nbsp;
care much for this area. The Avar Khagan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.400
seeing this golden opportunity to gain&nbsp;
more land, especially since most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.200
Byzantine forces were preoccupied fighting the&nbsp;
Persians in the east, agreed to this alliance.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.000
What’s interesting is that it seems like the Avar&nbsp;
Khaganate before the siege of Constantinople was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.200
going through some political turmoil. Since we&nbsp;
have no written records from the Avars it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.880
hard to say what this political turmoil was but it&nbsp;
seems like the prestigiou base rule of the Khagan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.800
as present in all nomadic kingdoms, was getting&nbsp;
challenged due to some reason mostly by the Slavs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.720
and Bulgars. In fact this may have been the reason&nbsp;
why the Khagan agreed on breaking the truce and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.840
allying himself with the Persians, and why he&nbsp;
decided on taking Constantinople right away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.880
instead of taking smaller cities in the Balkans&nbsp;
first. The Khagan needed a big victory with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.680
lot of booty to solidify his control among&nbsp;
the many peoples in the Avar Khaganate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.160
taking Constantinople would do that. Also&nbsp;
he probably didn’t want to give much time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.320
for Heraclius to return with his army&nbsp;
and so went straight for Constantinople.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.600
The Avar preparations for the siege began in&nbsp;
early 626. The Avar Khagan menaged to assemble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.120
a formidable force with the core of the army&nbsp;
being formed by heavy Avar cavalry/infantry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.080
which was supported by the&nbsp;
Bulgar cavalry/infantry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:21.120
the rest was light infantry being mostly&nbsp;
Slavic and Gepid. The Avar army arrived at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.720
Adrianople and waited there for at least a month&nbsp;
partaking in phoney warfare with the Byztinees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.360
This may have been either because the Avars were&nbsp;
still waiting for parts of their army to show up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.000
or they were waiting for the first fruits of the&nbsp;
harvest to finish so they had enough supplies.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.720
Nevertheless the vanguard of the Avar army&nbsp;
crossed the Anastasian Wall on the 29th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:46.000
of July reaching the formidable Theodosian&nbsp;
Walls that same day. The Anastasian wall was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.240
built by Emperor Anastasian in the late&nbsp;
5th century in order to prevent nomadic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.480
groups like the Avars from raiding the&nbsp;
suburbs of Constantinople but at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.440
point the wall was kind of dilapidated and&nbsp;
the Avar Army had no problem crossing it.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.680
With most of the Byzantine army and the&nbsp;
Emperor Heraclius campaigning in the East,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:07.200
Constantinople was pretty&nbsp;
much left to defend itself.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.240
This was a momentous occasion. It was&nbsp;
the first time in almost 250 years that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.040
Constantinople came under siege and I would&nbsp;
argue it was actually even longer than that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.000
because the Gothic siege of the city in 378&nbsp;
wasn’t really a siege but I digress. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.280
also the first time the formidable Theodosian&nbsp;
triple layered walls would be put to the test.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.920
Before we go further it must be stated that&nbsp;
all the accounts we have from the siege of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.760
Constantinople are from the Byzantine&nbsp;
point of view which means we end up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.520
writings about the siege that look like this.
‘The goal of war is in general the hope of booty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:47.760
the removal of men, the seizure of goods; this is&nbsp;
why the barbarian peoples begin a war … What other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:54.400
but our city would rightly be that which could be&nbsp;
named infallibly and correctly, in its totality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.920
the place of sacrifice of God, and one can see&nbsp;
that it is generally speaking the only church&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.760
which sings the glory and the anthems of God&nbsp;
and the Virgin … Thus … we should interpret the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.120
word "Gog" (from the bible) for the assembly&nbsp;
of these people led against us by this mad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.720
dog [the Khagan].’ (Theodore the Syncellus,&nbsp;
Homolly on the Siege of Constantinople 43,44)

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:18.320
Now the word ‘Gog’ is taken from the bible and&nbsp;
it means an apocalyptic horde of barbarians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.200
of biblical proportions that the devout&nbsp;
christian Byzantines are defending against,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.120
in the name of god and the Virgin&nbsp;
Mary. Hence Constantinople is seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.040
by the Byzantines as this quote&nbsp;
‘place of sacrifice of god’.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:37.600
So the sources we have almost unanimously present&nbsp;
this narrative of a small pious force defending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:44.000
against this much larger barbaric pagan enemy. The&nbsp;
problem with this view is that even though yes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.480
numbers of the Avar army were most certainly&nbsp;
much larger than the numbers of the Byzantine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.480
defenders, the Byzantines were defending probably&nbsp;
at this point the most fortified city on earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.520
with the Byzantine Navy at their disposal. Plus&nbsp;
it’s not like the nomadic peoples where well known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.320
for their siege ability and the Avars with the&nbsp;
Slavs in particular where already unable to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.520
the much smaller fortified city of Thessaloniki&nbsp;
just couple of years prior, so this desperate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.480
narrative of the siege in our sources is at the&nbsp;
very least quite exaggerated by the Byzantines.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:19.360
All this brings us back to the first&nbsp;
day of the siege on the 29th of July&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.600
as the Avar army is setting up in front&nbsp;
of the triple layered Theodosian Walls.&nbsp;

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:29.200
The first two days of the siege involved various&nbsp;
diplomatic actions, plus psychological warfare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.680
The Byzantines peraded upon their walls&nbsp;
Christian Relics while the Avars held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:38.400
military parades. Both actions were supposed&nbsp;
to encourage their own soldiers while scaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:44.320
their enemies. The Avars during this time also&nbsp;
started setting up siege towers, turtles, etc.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:49.040
Now, if you look at the map of Constantinople&nbsp;
you notice this body of water surrounding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.120
city from the north. This is called the&nbsp;
Golden Horn and it provides a very good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:57.680
harbour and natural barrier for defending&nbsp;
the city. However, since there already is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:02.800
this natural barrier the fortifications on&nbsp;
this side of the city were quite lackluster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.280
It is still debated to what extent this&nbsp;
area was fortified but all historians agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.400
at the very least it definitely wasn’t as well&nbsp;
fortified as the western Theodosian Walls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:17.120
Therefore, if Constantinople had a&nbsp;
weak point, this was that weak point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.840
and the Khagan obviously knew this because&nbsp;
he brought with him a quote “navy”.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.400
This Avar navy was manned by the Slavs. The&nbsp;
Byzantines wrote that the Slavs brought with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.960
over land dugout canoes most likely&nbsp;
being ordered to do so by the Khagan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:39.920
Now the word used by the Byzantines for the slavic&nbsp;
boats is “monoxil” which mostly describes dugout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.960
canoes but sometimes can mean more complex&nbsp;
boats in Byznatine texts. Therefore some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:50.480
historians try to say that the Slavs had actual&nbsp;
battle ready well constructed boats with masts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.720
but considering the extent of Slavic naval&nbsp;
experience, the fact that they brought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.440
boats over land rather than sailing down the&nbsp;
Black sea, and that similar dugout canoes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:04.640
used during the already mentioned unsuccessful&nbsp;
siege of Thessaloniki, means that it is probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:09.280
safe to say that these boats were normal dugout&nbsp;
canoes that could have been slightly modified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:14.160
for siege warfare, or even strung together for&nbsp;
better sailing potential but that’s about it.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.880
On the 3rd day of the siege 31st of July,&nbsp;
an all out Avar assault happened across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:26.560
theodosian walls. The first wave of the attack&nbsp;
was done by the quote ‘naked slavs’ with ladders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:30.480
this didn’t do much damage but after&nbsp;
the defenders were weakened slightly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.760
the heavy infantry replaced them. This first&nbsp;
extensive Avar attack didn’t go too well but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:40.480
it wasn’t supposed to. The Avars did not commit&nbsp;
their siege weapons during this attack and so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.280
is most likely the Khagan just ordered this&nbsp;
attack to probe the defences of the city.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:50.000
On the fourth day of the siege 1st of August.&nbsp;
The day started with the Avar catapults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.960
pummeling the middle of the theodosian walls, the&nbsp;
defenders deployed their own catapults and were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.080
much more skilled in aiming them than the Avars&nbsp;
and so the battle of the catapults ended up being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:04.960
an Avar defeat. This did not matter to the Avars&nbsp;
much, as they then brought out their siege towers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:09.600
that made their way to the walls. But even here&nbsp;
the Byzantines had a trick up their sleeves. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:15.440
of the sailors created a weird long crane with a&nbsp;
small boat at the end filled with flammable fluid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.320
it is speculated that this fluid could have been&nbsp;
the predecessor to Greek fire but we don’t know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.960
that for sure. Nevertheless this contraption&nbsp;
proved to be quite useful in destroying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:30.080
siege towers and the Avars were defeated in their&nbsp;
attack ones more. However, despite this defeat at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:34.880
the walls the Avars did succeed somewhere else.&nbsp;
While the Byzantines were busy defending the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:40.080
the Slavs managed to launch all of their dugout&nbsp;
canoes at the edge of the Golden Horn, here they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:44.640
were protected from the Byzantine navy as the&nbsp;
water was too shallow for the large Byzatine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:49.040
boats to engage the canoes effectively,&nbsp;
and so the fourth day of the siege ended.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.520
On the fifth day of the Siege the Khagan stopped&nbsp;
hostilities and sent out for a Byzatine embassy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:00.240
to meet him in front of the walls. When the&nbsp;
embassy arrived the Khagan showed them three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:05.040
Persian dignitaries who were there in support&nbsp;
of the Khagan, the Khagan then said that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:10.080
Persian army was waiting on the other side of&nbsp;
the Bosporus to assist the Avars in their siege.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:15.040
This wasn’t the full Persian army, that one was&nbsp;
preoccupied fighting Heraclius, no, this was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:20.880
a contingent of soldiers send around Herclius’s&nbsp;
main force as to assist the Avars in their siege.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.800
This Persian army was actually on the&nbsp;
other side of the Bosporus since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:29.120
start of the siege on the 29th of July and&nbsp;
the Byzantines were aware of them. But it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:33.520
only now that the Khagan used this&nbsp;
Persian army as a fear mongering tactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:39.280
stating how many men he had and how certain it&nbsp;
was Constantinople would fall. The Khagan told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:45.440
the embassy, “Leave me your city and property. For&nbsp;
otherwise it is impossible for you to be saved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:50.240
unless you can become fish and depart&nbsp;
by sea, or birds and ascend to the sky.”

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:54.320
The Byzantines were not moved by&nbsp;
these words sarcastically retorting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:58.160
“Although you have such great&nbsp;
hordes, you still need Persian help.”&nbsp;

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:02.560
Seeing that the Byzantines would not surrender,&nbsp;
the Khagan broke off the negotiations.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:07.520
The following night the three Persian dignitaries&nbsp;
crossing the Bosporus back to the Persian army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.760
were caught by Byzantines Patrol boats.&nbsp;
The Byzantines beheaded one of the Persians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.880
then tied his head to the neck of the&nbsp;
other Persian who’s hands they cut off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:20.640
after which they send him outside of&nbsp;
the city walls back to the Khagan.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:25.600
The third dignitary was beheaded on a boat in&nbsp;
view of the Persian army on the other side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:31.280
Bosporus. After that the byzantines through the&nbsp;
head to the Persians with an attached note saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:36.880
“The Chagan, after making terms with us, sent us&nbsp;
the ambassadors who were dispatched to him by you;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:41.600
we have beheaded two of them in the city, while&nbsp;
look! You have the head of the third one.”

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:45.920
The Persians did not fall for this trick&nbsp;
as on the night of the 6th day of the siege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:50.160
the remaining Slavic canoes not in the&nbsp;
Golden horn made their way to the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.320
side of the bosporus and picked up the&nbsp;
Persian army. However, on the way back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:59.440
they were ambushed by the much superior&nbsp;
Byantine navy and most of them were killed.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.920
So ends the Persian chapter of the siege.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:08.320
On the 7th and 8th day of the siege there were&nbsp;
minor attacks here and there across the wall but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:13.280
no major attack came. The only major development&nbsp;
was that the Khagan moved to be positioned on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:18.000
the other side of the Golden Horn rather than&nbsp;
in front of the main walls. It was obvious the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.120
Avars were planning something, something big,&nbsp;
and so the 9th day of the siege rolled around.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:30.560
On this day, in early morning, an all out attack&nbsp;
was conducted by the Avar army across the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:35.680
wall. This attack only intensified as the&nbsp;
day dragged on and at some point in the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:41.840
the Khagan ordered the Slavic fleet which was now&nbsp;
bolstered by Burglar infantry on board the canoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.200
to do a full scale attack on&nbsp;
the north side of the city.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:50.240
Here the canoes met with the Byzantine navy&nbsp;
across the entire golden horn and a slog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:56.000
fest of fighting from boat to boat started. It&nbsp;
did not take long for the Byzantines to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:02.720
the upper hand and the entire quote “Slavic Navy”&nbsp;
was destroyed. Accounts say that the Golden Horn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:07.520
turned red from all the bloodshed. The few&nbsp;
Slavs that managed to make it back ashore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:12.960
were reportedly killed by the Avars for losing&nbsp;
the battle. Seeing that the fleet was defeated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:17.680
the rest of the Slavs and Bulgars and&nbsp;
other people fighting under the Avar banner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:23.200
started to withdraw from the siege. It is reported&nbsp;
that some of the Slavs fled into the local hills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:27.760
presumably settling somewhere within&nbsp;
the Balkans, others went back home.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:33.200
The Khagan still stayed encamped outside of the&nbsp;
city but on the 10th day of the siege even he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:38.960
withdrew seeing that it was now impossible to take&nbsp;
Constantinople with his entire fleet defeated.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:43.120
Byzantium won, they completely&nbsp;
destroyed the prestigious base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:47.920
rule of the khagan and the Avar khaganate&nbsp;
would never fully recover from this defeat.&nbsp;

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:53.600
After the siege the Avars had to deal with a&nbsp;
plethora of internal problems and rebellions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:58.880
in the west with Samo, in the East with&nbsp;
Kubrat, in the south with many Slavic warlords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:03.520
with Lombard encroachment and with the&nbsp;
Byzantine efforts of retaking the Balkans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:10.000
The Avars would eventually after about 150 years&nbsp;
solidify their control in Pannonia ones more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:15.040
but they would never reach the height or power&nbsp;
they enjoyed before the siege of Constantinople.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:19.120
So goes the story of the Medieval Slavic Navy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.160
the first medieval siege of Constantinople&nbsp;
and the Avar decline of the 7th century.

